217: | 1 | Reading code closely. *How does each part of a program do its job, and how do we use code other people wrote?* Anchor: a working twenty-line guessing game, run once, then read line by line | 12 | g10-analyze-code; g10-apis-docs | HS-PRO-RD-17; HS-PRO-PD-13 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT6 (say what each part is for); CT7 (see a procedure as a reusable part); CT9 (use the debugger to test and refine); ESR2 (credit the library and its documentation); IC5 (driver and navigator switch at the halfway signal) | Lesson 1: the anchor run once; hook question; retrieval trace of g9-modular; readiness check (last ten minutes) on reading a saved code sample against tests (g8-evaluate-ai-code) and writing a credit line (g8-attribution). Lesson 2 (unplugged): a procedure call traced on a trace-table, the parameter and the return value each in a column; the computer does exactly what is written, in order. Lesson 3 (device): a list passed into a procedure, the list-diagram drawn before and after, stepped through in the editor. Lesson 4: the whole anchor explained part by part, earlier years' parts (sequence, loop, condition, variable, procedure) and this year's (parameter, return value, list) and how they work together: the variable holds the secret number, the while loop runs until the guess is right, the if and else give the feedback. Lesson 5 (modify): change one argument or one return line and predict, run, compare. Lesson 6 (device): one saved code sample read, traced and run against a test-table, a first look at HS-PRO-RD-18 (owned in grade 11, g11-evaluate-generated-code); no live tool. Lesson 7 (unplugged): reading a documentation page: what a function takes, what it returns, its example; a library's interface (its API: the functions it offers and how each is called) as the promise the documentation makes. Lesson 8 (device): a feature added to a program with a supplied offline game library (its dice roll is a probabilistic step, reviewed from grade 9) from its documentation (paper path: the call written from the printed page, part met without a device). Lesson 9 (device): the editor's step-through debugger and its highlighting used as development tools to find a bug; the library and its page credited in a comment above the call, a first look at HS-PRO-PD-14 (owned in grade 12, g12-attribution). Lesson 10 (make): each pair adds a second feature using the library, tests it and writes what they tested and what they changed. Out: no program with many connected data structures, no complex algorithm analysis, no writing a library or an API, no live generated-code tool. End-of-unit (lesson 11): a sitting with unseen items: explain the parts of a new fifteen-line program and trace one call with a list (HS-PRO-RD-17), and from a new printed documentation page write and run one library call in a given program, with its credit comment (HS-PRO-PD-13; paper version marked part met without a device); respond lesson 12. Tools: our own editor (Python text view and step-through trace, job: run, trace and debug the programs); a small offline game library (dice rolls, shuffled decks, a score board) with its one-page documentation (job: the library students read and use). Curio Class writes: the anchor program and its tested solution key, the trace-table and list-diagram sheets, the saved code sample with its test-table, the library and its documentation page, the readiness check items | First because every later code reading in the year (unit 2's searches, unit 4's cleaning steps) asks students to explain a call with parameters and a return value, and it builds directly on grade 9's procedures and lists (g9-modular, g9-structures-program) while they are fresh. Reading comes before using a library, since the documentation describes a call in the same terms. The readiness check sits here because the two grade 8 rows it covers return in this unit | Candidate: kept, a one-page "if you are stuck" sheet for the teacher on the three commonest bugs of the anchor, zero added days | Pair work: roles named and switched, no student at the keyboard all lesson. The guessing game uses numbers only, no names. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
218: | 2 | Which algorithm is better? *When two algorithms solve the same task, how do we decide which is better, and which kind of AI suits a task?* Anchor: finding one name in a sorted list of a thousand made-up names, once by checking every name and once by halving the list | 9 | g10-evaluate-algorithms; g10-choose-ai-type | HS-ALG-PS-03; HS-ALG-ML-06 | Concept area: algorithms and design (with AI inside it). Practices: CT9 (test cases for correctness); CT7 (see what grows with the input); IC3 (justify a choice to a partner); IC5 (pair roles) | Lesson 1: the anchor as two pseudocode versions; retrieval trace of g9's pseudocode. Lesson 2 (unplugged): both searches stepped through on number cards, steps counted for lists of 8, 16 and 32. Lesson 3 (device): both run in the editor with a step counter on lists of 10, 100, 1,000 and 10,000 and the counts put in a table; the halving search's count grows far more slowly. Lesson 4 (modify): correctness by test cases (the first item, the last, a missing item, an empty list): a halving search with a bug is fixed and retested. Lesson 5 (modify): clarity: two correct versions compared for how easily a reader follows them, and one rewritten with clearer names; memory use named as a second cost. Lesson 6 (unplugged): retrieval of g9-ai-tour; for task cards (sort messages, group shoppers by habit, predict a bus's arrival, caption a photo), the data each task has and what an error costs. Lesson 7 (application): students justify an AI type for a new task in two sentences, naming its strengths and limits for the task: the data's size and type, whether a person can follow how it decides, the outcome wanted; a choice that suits a different task is the wrong example. Out: no Big O notation, no proofs, no analysis of all inputs, no logarithms (the halving is counted, never written as a formula), and no mathematics or building of the AI types (built in grade 11). End-of-unit (lesson 8): a sitting with unseen items: for a new pair of algorithms (two ways to find the largest number in a list), count steps on given sizes, find the test case one version fails and say which is clearer and why (HS-ALG-PS-03); justify an AI type for a new task card in two sentences (HS-ALG-ML-06); respond lesson 9, whose last ten minutes hold the packet-strip readiness item for unit 3. Tools: our own editor with a step counter (job: run both searches on growing lists and count). Curio Class writes: the two searches and their tested keys, the number cards, the buggy halving search, the AI task cards with their keys | After unit 1 because comparing the searches needs a call with a list read and traced. Before units 4 and 6 because the test-case habit and the AI types return there (the cleaning steps are tested; the human-choices case is partly a data-driven system). Comparing algorithms and choosing an AI type sit together as one question: which way of solving suits the task | Candidate: kept, a step-count table template so pairs record counts the same way, zero added days | Made-up names only. Number work stays inside earlier math: counting and comparing counts as a list doubles, no logarithm. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
219: | 3 | Networks and breaches. *How does a message cross the internet, and what happens to people when a system is broken into?* Anchor: what happens, step by step, when a student in a small office loads one page from a server across town | 8 | g10-network-diagram; g10-network-of-networks; g10-breach-causes | HS-SYS-NT-34; HS-SYS-NT-35; HS-SYS-SE-32 | Concept area: systems and security. Practices: CT7 (a network as a model of parts and roles); CT8 (plan a diagram before drawing it); CT6 (classify a breach by cause); ESR2 (whose data is exposed) | Lesson 1: the anchor; retrieval of g9-network-sketch (read a given diagram). Lesson 2 (unplugged): a small office network diagrammed with its hardware (devices, a router, a server, storage) and its software (the operating system layer of grade 9's system-diagram and the programs on each), and one request traced through it, each part's role named. Lesson 3 (application): a network drawn from a written description, then checked by a partner who traces a request through it. Lesson 4: several small networks joined on the map; a packet strip traced between two of them through routers; addresses. Lesson 5: the internet as a network of networks: nobody runs the whole, many networks agree on shared rules (TCP/IP named), clients and servers, and the layers a message passes through at a high level; a school network and the internet compared on structure, size, addressing and how data moves. Lesson 6 (unplugged): retrieval of g9-security-measures; breach and scam case cards sorted by cause (a weak password, a trick that fooled a person, an unpatched system), each placed on the cia-triangle. Lesson 7: one breach, many groups: an invented hospital breach (invented for practice) classified by its impact on individuals (patients), an industry (the hospital), a community (the local health system) and a government (the rules that apply), and by kind (money, trust, legal, the work that stops). Lesson 8: a second case classified from supplied government pages (see the last column's sources), then review. Out: no building or configuring a network, no packet analysis, no protocol writing, no attack steps or forensic detail, no professional security tools. End-of-unit: the mid-year course-level check (lessons 30 and 31 of the course) stands for it. Tools: none (paper diagrams; a toy network page shown on one screen at most). Curio Class writes: the office network description cards, the packet strips, the breach and scam case cards with their keys, the invented hospital case | After units 1 and 2 because the client and server roles are read as programs talking (unit 1's calls), and before the mid-year check so the check scores a whole unit. Diagram before network of networks (one network, then many), and networks before breaches (a breach needs the parts it broke into). Placed in weeks 11 to 15, after the core check weeks 8 to 11 except its first lesson | Candidate: kept, a one-page diagram key of symbols (device, router, server, link) so every diagram reads the same, zero added days | Scams can touch a student's family: no student is asked whether anyone they know was scammed; a student's own offer is welcome and the teacher page carries health's "If a student tells you something" steps word for word. Cases are told by what happened, no company named. Sources for the teacher line: the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services Office for Civil Rights breach report page (ocrportal.hhs.gov/ocr/breach), counts by breach type as of the day the unit is written; the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency's "Avoiding Social Engineering and Phishing Attacks" page (cisa.gov); the Federal Trade Commission's phishing page (consumer.ftc.gov). No attack is run. Agent reads: sensitivity. |
