data/progressions/math.csv:4:counting-cardinality,K,mk-one-to-one,K.CC.B.4a,,m1-count-to-120,,,count; touch; one each,,"Count objects by pairing each word with one and only one object, using a pointing or moving act to keep word and object together (Progressions, PDF 16 (printed 10)). Small collections first (to 5, then 10), objects in a line before scattered ones. Typical error: skipping or double-counting an object, saying words faster than touches. Feeds mk-how-many in the same grade and hands on to m1-count-to-120, where grade 1 counts large collections one by one before grouping them in tens. Cluster K.CC.B, major. IM Kindergarten unit 1 (Math in Our World, sections B and D, counting collections)."
data/progressions/math.csv:5:counting-cardinality,K,mk-how-many,K.CC.B.4; K.CC.B.4b; ~K.CC.B.5; ~K.CC.A.3,,m1-count-on,,ten-frame,how many; last number; same; zero,,"Owns the parent K.CC.B.4 (connect counting to cardinality; its parts a and c sit in mk-one-to-one and mk-one-more). The last number said tells how many, and the count does not change when the objects are moved or counted in another order (Progressions, PDF 17 (printed 11)). Count to answer how many and count out a given number, within 10 in this row: objects in a line, an array or a circle, and scattered sets; write the numerals 0 to 10 (0 for an empty set). Introduces the 5-frame and the 10-frame (ten-frame) as a structured place for 10 objects. Starts K.CC.B.5 and K.CC.A.3, which mk-count-to-20 completes to 20. Typical error: answering how many by recounting from 1 or by repeating the whole count. Cluster K.CC.B (and K.CC.A), major. IM Kindergarten unit 2 (Numbers 1 to 10), sections A to C."
data/progressions/math.csv:9:counting-cardinality,K,mk-count-to-20,K.CC.A.3; K.CC.B.5,,m1-count-to-120,ten-frame,ten-frame,eleven to twenty; teen number; count out; numeral,,"Completes K.CC.B.5 and K.CC.A.3 from mk-how-many: count to tell how many with up to 20 objects in a line, an array or a circle, or up to 10 scattered; count out a given number from 1 to 20; write numerals 0 to 20 and match them to collections. Limits are the standard's own (20 arranged, 10 scattered); counting a circle needs a start mark, an array needs row-by-row tracking (Progressions, PDF 16 (printed 10)). Two ten-frames show teen collections. Cluster K.CC.B and K.CC.A, major. IM Kindergarten unit 6 (Numbers 0 to 20), sections A and C."
data/progressions/math.csv:181:ratios-proportions,7,m7-proportional-graphs,7.RP.A.2d; ~7.RP.A.2a,m6-four-quadrants; m6-equivalent-ratios,m8-slope-similar-triangles; m8-proportional-compare; m8-function-compare; m8-function-qualitative; m8-scatter-plots,coordinate-plane; ratio-table,coordinate-plane,origin; straight line; point; unit rate; steepness,,"IM 7.2, lessons 11 to 15; cluster 7.RP.A, major. Students graph proportional relationships, recognise them as straight lines through the origin (the graph test of 7.RP.A.2a), and explain what a point (x, y) means in the situation, with special attention to (0, 0) and (1, r) where r is the unit rate. Compares several relationships on one set of axes (steeper means a greater unit rate). Limit: first quadrant, nonnegative values; slope as a word waits for grade 8. Progressions RP 6-7, PDF page 170 (printed 164)."
data/progressions/math.csv:200:statistics-probability,7,m7-compare-populations,7.SP.B.3; 7.SP.B.4,m6-center-variability; m6-data-displays,m8-scatter-plots; alg1-data-displays; alg1-center-spread,dot-plot; box-plot,dot-plot; box-plot,overlap; difference in centers; mean absolute deviation; compare; inference; population,,"IM 7.8, lessons 11 and 18 (lesson 19, the information-gap practice, is an extension), with sampling preparation in lessons 12 to 16; cluster 7.SP.B, additional. Students judge the visual overlap of two distributions with similar spread and express the difference in centers as a multiple of a measure of variability (centers 2 MADs apart is a meaningful difference), then use centers and variability from random samples to draw informal comparative inferences about two populations (are seventh-grade books longer than fourth-grade books?). Limit: informal only, no formal tests. Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 199 (printed 193)."
data/progressions/math.csv:216:statistics-probability,8,m8-scatter-plots,8.SP.A.1; 8.SP.A.2,m7-compare-populations; m7-proportional-graphs,alg1-scatter-linear-models; alg1-correlation-causation,coordinate-plane; graph-of-function,scatter-plot; coordinate-plane,scatter plot; association; positive; negative; linear; nonlinear; cluster; outlier; line of fit,science:MS-LS1-5 (scatter plots of growth against a condition),"IM 8.6 (Associations in Data), lessons 1 to 8; cluster 8.SP.A, supporting (it practises the linear relationships of 8.EE.B, taught in grade 8 unit 3; function language is joined to it in unit 6). Students make and read scatter plots of bivariate measurement data, describe clustering, outliers, positive or negative and linear or nonlinear association, and informally fit a straight line where the association looks linear, judging the fit by how close the points are to it. Introduces the scatter-plot. Limit: lines fit by eye, no least squares (high school). Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 200 (printed 194)."
data/progressions/math.csv:217:statistics-probability,8,m8-linear-models-data,8.SP.A.3,m7-proportional-equations,alg1-scatter-linear-models,scatter-plot; graph-of-function,scatter-plot,linear model; slope; intercept; predict; interpret,,"IM 8.6, lessons 5 to 8; cluster 8.SP.A, supporting. Students use the equation of a fitted linear model to answer questions and interpret its slope and intercept in context (""a slope of 1.5 cm per hour means each extra hour of sunlight goes with about 1.5 cm more plant growth""). Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 200 (printed 194)."
data/progressions/math.csv:218:statistics-probability,8,m8-two-way-tables,8.SP.A.4,m7-probability-models; m7-chance-probability,alg1-two-way-tables,,two-way-table,two-way table; categorical data; frequency; relative frequency; row; column; association,social-studies:ss8-election-info (percents in polling data),"IM 8.6, lessons 9 to 10; cluster 8.SP.A, supporting. Students build two-way tables of frequencies and relative frequencies for two categorical variables on the same subjects (curfew and chores), compute row or column relative frequencies, and decide whether the two variables seem associated. Introduces the two-way-table. Progressions SP 6-8, PDF page 200 (printed 194). Teacher note: survey questions avoid personal or sensitive topics. Relative frequency comes from m7-chance-probability (7.SP.C.6); m7-probability-models adds models from observed frequencies."
data/progressions/math.csv:225:statistics-probability,9,alg1-data-displays,S-ID.A.1,m7-compare-populations; m6-data-displays,alg2-normal-distributions; stat-quantitative-distributions,dot-plot; histogram; box-plot; number-line,dot-plot; histogram; box-plot,statistical question; numerical data; categorical data; distribution; symmetric; skewed; bimodal; uniform,science:HS-LS2-2 (biology compares survey data shown in plots),"Represent one numerical variable with dot plots, histograms and box plots, choose the display that fits the question, and describe the distribution by shape (symmetric, skewed left or right, bimodal, uniform). The course opens with class survey data and published data; students sort statistical from non-statistical questions and numerical from categorical data. Growth from grades 6 and 7: the same three displays, now chosen on purpose and read for shape before any number is computed. Numbers: real data sets of about 10 to 100 values, technology for the larger ones. Limits: one variable at a time; shape words only, no density curves (normal distributions wait for grade 11). IM Alg1.1 lessons 1 to 5 (and 6 to 8 for spreadsheet work). Progressions: shape, centre and spread from 6 to 8 deepened (pdf 283 (p 277)). Modeling: every Statistics and Probability standard is a modeling standard (pdf 282 (p 276)), so data work recurs in grades 10 and 11. Skips grade 10 on purpose: Geometry carries no algebra or functions unit, so Algebra II builds on Algebra I directly (IM course order Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II). Assumes m7-compare-populations (grade 7) and m6-data-displays (grade 6) across grade 8 on purpose: grade 8 statistics is bivariate only (scatter plots and two-way tables), so one-variable displays were last taught in grades 6 and 7, and histograms last in grade 6. Dot plots, histograms and box plots return here after grade 8, whose statistics is bivariate only. Re-entry (revisited): rebuild m6-data-displays, m7-compare-populations briefly before using it, opening with a readiness check; fluent recall after 2 grades without the skill is not assumed. Reactivation (box-plot, unused in grade 8): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work. Reactivation (dot-plot, unused in grade 8): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work. Reactivation (histogram, unused in grade 7, 8): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:233:statistics-probability,9,alg1-two-way-tables,S-ID.B.5,m8-two-way-tables,geo-two-way-probability; geo-addition-rule; stat-categorical-data; stat-chi-square,two-way-table,two-way-table,two-way table; joint frequency; marginal frequency; relative frequency; conditional relative frequency; association,,"Summarise two categorical variables in a two-way frequency table, compute joint, marginal and conditional relative frequencies (by row and by column), and use them to say whether the data suggest an association. Grade 8 two-way tables grow into relative frequencies chosen on purpose: which total is the right denominator for the question asked. Numbers: counts up to a few hundred, percents. Limits: association only, no probability language yet (that is grade 10, geo-two-way-probability, which applies this standard again). IM Alg1.3 lessons 1 to 3. Progressions: S-ID.5 (pdf 284 (p 278))."
data/progressions/math.csv:234:statistics-probability,9,alg1-scatter-linear-models,S-ID.B.6; S-ID.B.6b; S-ID.B.6c; S-ID.C.7; ~S-ID.B.6a; ~N-Q.A.3,m8-scatter-plots; m8-linear-models-data,alg2-combining-functions-models; stat-correlation-regression; stat-regression-inference,scatter-plot; coordinate-plane,scatter-plot,scatter plot; line of best fit; residual; slope; intercept; predict; linear model,,"Represent two quantitative variables on a scatter plot, fit a line (by eye, then by technology), interpret its slope and intercept in context, and judge the fit by plotting and reading residuals. Students predict with the model and say when a prediction is unreasonable (far outside the data). Grade 8 informal lines of fit become models with units and residuals. Numbers: real data with decimals, rounded to a level the measurements support (N-Q.A.3, owned in grade 10). Limits: linear fits here; fitting exponential and quadratic functions to data (S-ID.B.6a) is started in Alg1.4 and Alg1.5 and completed in grade 11 (alg2-combining-functions-models). IM Alg1.3 lessons 4 to 6. Progressions: least squares line fit by technology (pdf 284 (p 278)). Skips grade 10 on purpose: Geometry carries no algebra or functions unit, so Algebra II builds on Algebra I directly (IM course order Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II)."
data/progressions/math.csv:235:statistics-probability,9,alg1-correlation-causation,S-ID.C.8; S-ID.C.9,m8-scatter-plots,alg2-study-design-randomness; stat-correlation-regression,scatter-plot,scatter-plot,correlation coefficient; strong; weak; positive; negative; causation; lurking variable,ela:e9-ri-argument-evaluate (judge whether evidence supports a causal claim); science:s7-climate-change-evidence (revisit earlier qualitative reasoning about correlation and cause),"Compute the correlation coefficient with technology, read it as a measure of how closely the points cluster around a line (near 1 or -1 strong, near 0 weak), and distinguish correlation from causation: a strong association does not show that one variable causes the other, and a third variable can drive both. Students critique causal claims in short reports. Limits: r is interpreted, never computed by hand; causation is argued only from how the data were collected (randomised experiments are grade 11, alg2-study-design-randomness). IM Alg1.3 lessons 7 to 10. Progressions: S-ID.9, a third variable behind both (pdf 286 (p 280)). Numbers: correlation coefficients between -1 and 1 read to two decimal places, from technology. Skips grade 10 on purpose: Geometry carries no algebra or functions unit, so Algebra II builds on Algebra I directly (IM course order Algebra I, Geometry, Algebra II). Retrieve the earlier distinction from grade 7 science, then teach the correlation coefficient and the limits of statistical association as new mathematics."
data/progressions/math.csv:298:functions,11,alg2-combining-functions-models,F-BF.A.1; F-BF.A.1b; S-ID.B.6a,alg1-scatter-linear-models; alg1-quadratic-patterns,pc-composition; stat-residuals-transformations,scatter-plot; graph-of-function,,model; fit; combine functions; sum of functions; data; transformation of a model,,"Write functions that describe relationships between quantities, combine standard function types with arithmetic (a cooling drink as a constant plus a decaying exponential), and fit a function to data by choosing a family suggested by the context and transforming it to match, then use the model to answer questions in context. Completes F-BF.A.1 and S-ID.B.6a (linear, quadratic and exponential fits), started in Algebra I. Numbers: real data, technology. Limits: given or chosen families; regression beyond technology fits is not required. IM Alg2.5 lessons 10 to 11 (and 1, 3, 7, 8 for fitting). Modeling: F-BF.A.1 is a modeling standard; data modeling recurs from Algebra I units 3 and 5. Algebra II builds on Algebra I across Geometry. Actual Geometry teaching and assessment can renew an earlier row; the verified Algebra II prerequisite ledger determines readiness or retrieval. The scatter plot returns here after Geometry, which has no data unit. Composing functions (F-BF.A.1c) is a (+) part listed in math-excluded.csv; combining here is by arithmetic only. F-BF.A.1 was started with quadratic patterns (alg1-quadratic-patterns). Re-entry: the verified Algebra II scope-and-sequence ledger sets readiness or retrieval for alg1-scatter-linear-models, alg1-quadratic-patterns; a student who misses it gets a brief reminder with the earlier model before the new work. Reactivation (scatter-plot, unused in grade 10): students draw and read one on a familiar example before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:313:functions,12,pc-exp-log-models,CUR-PC.5,alg2-number-e-continuous; alg2-exponential-growth-rational-inputs,,scatter-plot; graph-of-function,,logistic function; carrying capacity; semi-log plot; linearise; residual; half-life; doubling time,science:HS-LS2-1 (population growth that levels off at a carrying capacity),"Model data and situations with exponential, logarithmic and logistic functions; straighten curved data by taking logarithms of one or both variables and fit a line to the result; judge which model fits and how far it can be trusted. Biology's population graphs (bio-population-carrying-capacity, grade 9) give the logistic shape a context; half-life from Chemistry is a second. Numbers: real data sets, technology for fitting, a paper path with semi-log paper and a scientific calculator. Limits: logistic models are read and fitted, not derived (Calculus derives them by course order). Load: about 7 days, new; 1 reviews continuous growth. Re-entry: open with a short retrieval task on alg2-number-e-continuous, alg2-exponential-growth-rational-inputs (grade 11); a student who misses it gets a brief reminder with the earlier model before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:332:statistics-probability,12,stat-categorical-data,CUR-ST.1,alg1-two-way-tables,,two-way-table,,categorical variable; frequency table; relative frequency; bar chart; marginal distribution; conditional distribution; association,,"Summarise categorical data with frequency and relative frequency tables, bar charts and two-way tables, compute marginal and conditional relative frequencies, and describe whether two categorical variables are associated. Opens the course on Algebra I's two-way tables and Geometry's conditional probability read from them, now as distributions. Reactivation (two-way-table, unused in grade 11): one familiar table first. Numbers: real survey counts, percents. Load: about 5 days, about 2 of them review. Statistics stands beside Precalculus and Calculus at grade 12 and assumes none of them. Re-entry: open with a short readiness check on alg1-two-way-tables (grade 9, skips grades 10 and 11 apart from Geometry probability); a student who misses it gets a brief reminder with the earlier model before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:335:statistics-probability,12,stat-correlation-regression,CUR-ST.4,alg1-scatter-linear-models; alg1-correlation-causation,,scatter-plot,,explanatory variable; response variable; least-squares regression line; slope; intercept; correlation; coefficient of determination; extrapolation,,"Fit a least-squares regression line, interpret its slope and intercept in context, use the correlation and the coefficient of determination to describe a linear relationship, and predict without extrapolating far. Algebra I fitted lines with technology and computed r; new here are the least-squares idea (the line that makes the sum of squared residuals smallest), the formulas from means, standard deviations and r, and r squared. Load: about 7 days; 2 are review. Re-entry: a retrieval task inside a day on alg1-scatter-linear-models, alg1-correlation-causation (grade 9, used in grade 11 by alg2-combining-functions-models and alg2-study-design-randomness); a student who misses it gets a brief reminder with the earlier model before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:336:statistics-probability,12,stat-residuals-transformations,CUR-ST.5,alg2-combining-functions-models; alg2-logarithms,,scatter-plot,,residual; residual plot; influential point; outlier; high leverage; transformation; logarithmic transformation,science:HS-ESS3-5 (climate data and forecasts),"Judge a linear model with a residual plot, find outliers and influential points and describe their effect on the line, and straighten a curved relationship by transforming a variable (logarithm, square root, reciprocal) before fitting a line, then turn the fitted line back into a curve. Algebra I made residual plots (S-ID.B.6b) and Algebra II chose function families for data; this row adds influence and transformations. Chemistry's climate data (chem-climate-forecast, grade 10) give one data set. Load: about 6 days. Re-entry: a retrieval task inside a day on alg2-combining-functions-models, alg2-logarithms (grade 11); a student who misses it gets a brief reminder with the earlier model before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:352:statistics-probability,12,stat-regression-inference,CUR-ST.20,alg1-scatter-linear-models,,scatter-plot,,population regression line; slope; standard error of the slope; t interval for slope; t test for slope; computer output,science:HS-PS1-5 (reaction rate data against temperature or concentration),"Build a confidence interval and carry out a significance test for the slope of a population regression line, interpret them in context, checking conditions with residuals, and read the regression output of statistical software. Chemistry's rate data (chem-reaction-rates, grade 10) are one example. Builds on stat-correlation-regression and stat-residuals-transformations. Load: about 6 days. Re-entry: a retrieval task inside a day on alg1-scatter-linear-models (renewed in this course by stat-correlation-regression); a student who misses it gets a brief reminder with the earlier model before the new work."
data/progressions/math.csv:353:statistics-probability,12,stat-investigation-project,CUR-ST.21,,,dot-plot; histogram; scatter-plot,,statistical question; investigation; data collection plan; report; limitation,,"Plan and carry out a whole statistical investigation: pose a question, design the data collection, gather and analyse the data, choose and carry out an inference procedure, and report the findings and their limits in writing. Teacher note: questions avoid collecting sensitive personal data; surveys of classmates are anonymous and voluntary. Load: about 13 days, best spread through the year (a proposal after the design unit, data gathered during probability, analysis and report at the end), not one block. Assumes earlier Statistics rows only."
